WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.190
- As a board certified
family medicine doctor

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.240
with a special interest
in sports medicine,

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.450
I've seen and treated countless
musculoskeletal injuries.

00:00:07.450 --> 00:00:08.950
I've talked and guided patients

00:00:08.950 --> 00:00:10.410
through beginning workout regimens,

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.470
and I've created a list

00:00:11.470 --> 00:00:14.760
of Top 10 Beginner Mistakes
in Fitness Routines

00:00:18.249 --> 00:00:20.209
But for the people who
are trying to lose weight,

00:00:20.209 --> 00:00:22.179
they often fall into the
trap of thinking that

00:00:22.179 --> 00:00:24.829
exercise is the end all
and be all of weight loss.

00:00:24.829 --> 00:00:25.859
Unfortunately, it's not.

00:00:25.859 --> 00:00:28.011
70 to 80% of the weight loss that occurs

00:00:28.011 --> 00:00:30.789
happens as a result of the
foods that you take in.

00:00:30.789 --> 00:00:31.869
It's ideal to think

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:34.359
about your weight loss
journey in a holistic view,

00:00:34.359 --> 00:00:35.369
meaning that you should be thinking

00:00:35.369 --> 00:00:38.949
about recovery, sleep,
nutrition, and exercise.

00:00:38.949 --> 00:00:40.639
You can't just focus on one pillar

00:00:40.639 --> 00:00:42.119
and expect the pyramid to grow.

00:00:42.119 --> 00:00:46.399
The biggest fitness myth of
all time is spot reduction.

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:49.519
It doesn't exist. It's been
tested over and over again.

00:00:49.519 --> 00:00:51.119
You have to think about your nutrition,

00:00:51.119 --> 00:00:52.609
your exercise as a whole

00:00:52.609 --> 00:00:55.039
in order to decrease your
whole body fat content

00:00:55.039 --> 00:00:57.109
in order to see more muscular definition.

00:00:57.109 --> 00:00:59.589
This actually has been tested
in some really unique ways.

00:00:59.589 --> 00:01:02.699
Looking at tennis players,
their dominant forearm muscles

00:01:02.699 --> 00:01:05.269
in comparison to their
non-dominant hand, guess what?

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:07.129
A lot more muscle in their dominant hand,

00:01:07.129 --> 00:01:09.039
a lot less muscle in
their less dominant hand,

00:01:09.039 --> 00:01:10.859
fat content, exactly the same.

00:01:10.859 --> 00:01:13.719
It's incredible what I see
when I walk into a gym,

00:01:13.719 --> 00:01:15.639
the exercises that some people are doing

00:01:15.639 --> 00:01:17.329
and expecting to get results from.

00:01:17.329 --> 00:01:18.809
The simpler your workouts are,

00:01:18.809 --> 00:01:21.029
the more likely you are
to see long-term success.

00:01:21.029 --> 00:01:22.939
I'm talking about mixing in some cardio

00:01:22.939 --> 00:01:24.399
with some good old fashioned

00:01:24.399 --> 00:01:26.419
compound weightlifting movements.

00:01:26.419 --> 00:01:27.819
That's all you really need.

00:01:27.819 --> 00:01:29.379
And just cause I'm saying keep it simple,

00:01:29.379 --> 00:01:30.409
it doesn't mean that

00:01:30.409 --> 00:01:32.349
you can't have variety to your workouts.

00:01:32.349 --> 00:01:34.239
I love variety in my workouts.

00:01:34.239 --> 00:01:37.919
I'll rock climb, box, play
basketball, soccer, weightlift.

00:01:37.919 --> 00:01:41.109
There's so many ways to get
your physical activity in.

00:01:41.109 --> 00:01:42.449
As long as you're doing that,

00:01:42.449 --> 00:01:44.189
your routine really doesn't matter

00:01:44.189 --> 00:01:46.769
for beginner and just to
stay healthy, of course.

00:01:46.769 --> 00:01:50.419
You do not need to go on a shopping spree

00:01:50.419 --> 00:01:51.879
in the supplement store.

00:01:51.879 --> 00:01:54.419
You need to become consistent
first with your work workouts.

00:01:54.419 --> 00:01:56.279
You need to make sure
that you're following

00:01:56.279 --> 00:01:58.769
the right protocols, that
you're eating a healthy diet,

00:01:58.769 --> 00:02:00.299
that you're recovering correctly.

00:02:00.299 --> 00:02:03.469
There's such a small percentage
of change that occurs

00:02:03.469 --> 00:02:05.859
as a result of adding
supplements to your workouts.

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:07.009
And it's probably not worth

00:02:07.009 --> 00:02:08.959
even thinking about it so early on.

00:02:08.959 --> 00:02:11.789
And a very specific warning
I'd like to throw out there

00:02:11.789 --> 00:02:13.459
is when it comes to pre-workouts.

00:02:13.459 --> 00:02:15.399
These are usually loaded with caffeine

00:02:15.399 --> 00:02:17.759
and too many people take
them close to bedtime

00:02:17.759 --> 00:02:20.259
which destroys your sleep,
destroys your recovery.

00:02:20.259 --> 00:02:23.639
So your pre-workout is destroying
your post-workout gains.

00:02:23.639 --> 00:02:25.469
And if you do need a caffeine boost

00:02:25.469 --> 00:02:26.879
for a workout in the morning,

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:29.099
just have a cup of coffee or an espresso.

00:02:29.099 --> 00:02:30.819
You don't have to go for a pre-workout.

00:02:30.819 --> 00:02:34.199
This one, I see happen so often in gyms.

00:02:34.199 --> 00:02:35.969
When you're walking on a treadmill,

00:02:35.969 --> 00:02:38.139
running on a treadmill,
using an elliptical,

00:02:38.139 --> 00:02:40.459
do not hold the static grips.

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:41.839
Because essentially, what you're doing

00:02:41.839 --> 00:02:43.949
is decreasing the
intensity of the workout,

00:02:43.949 --> 00:02:45.759
meaning lower calories burn.

00:02:45.759 --> 00:02:47.539
You're decreasing the strain on your core

00:02:47.539 --> 00:02:49.919
making your core weaker,
hurting your posture.

00:02:49.919 --> 00:02:52.129
And if you're creating an
incline for your treadmill,

00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:55.279
by holding on, you're actually
decreasing that incline.

00:02:55.279 --> 00:02:57.419
So many benefits to letting your grip

00:02:57.419 --> 00:02:58.849
loosen up on those machines.

00:02:58.849 --> 00:03:01.469
When it comes to exercise,
goals are important

00:03:01.469 --> 00:03:03.559
and tracking can be interesting and fun

00:03:03.559 --> 00:03:07.069
but obsessing over these numbers
is truly counterproductive.

00:03:07.069 --> 00:03:08.242
10,000 steps a day.

00:03:08.242 --> 00:03:11.659
3,500 calories to be
burned to lose a pound.

00:03:11.659 --> 00:03:13.389
These numbers are really useful

00:03:13.389 --> 00:03:15.769
for statistic research purposes.

00:03:15.769 --> 00:03:18.749
They're not really meant for you to follow

00:03:18.749 --> 00:03:20.999
as a super strict set of guidelines.

00:03:20.999 --> 00:03:24.919
If you show up consistently,
that's gonna mean so much more

00:03:24.919 --> 00:03:27.449
than the intensity of
how hard you work out,

00:03:27.449 --> 00:03:29.079
how much weight you're actually lifting,

00:03:29.079 --> 00:03:30.609
how many calories you're burning.

00:03:30.609 --> 00:03:33.559
So forget about the numbers,
especially while starting off.

00:03:33.559 --> 00:03:35.949
Focus on showing up, having a good time,

00:03:37.679 --> 00:03:40.119
Too many times, I've
had patients and friends

00:03:40.119 --> 00:03:41.159
come up to me and tell me

00:03:41.159 --> 00:03:43.179
that they had an amazing first workout

00:03:43.179 --> 00:03:44.329
with a personal trainer

00:03:44.329 --> 00:03:47.089
because they went all out and
actually ended up getting sick

00:03:47.089 --> 00:03:48.299
that they worked out so hard.

00:03:48.299 --> 00:03:50.739
To me, this is a failure
on the trainers part.

00:03:50.739 --> 00:03:53.939
Never for your first workout
should you go all out.

00:03:53.939 --> 00:03:56.379
That creates increased risk for injury.

00:03:56.379 --> 00:03:59.059
You're less likely to feel
motivated going back to the gym.

00:03:59.059 --> 00:04:00.679
And even for me, when I've done this,

00:04:00.679 --> 00:04:02.999
I've gotten so sore
that I wasn't even able

00:04:02.999 --> 00:04:04.509
to go to the gym that same week.

00:04:04.509 --> 00:04:07.729
The whole idea of building
muscle, getting in shape

00:04:07.729 --> 00:04:09.749
is about creating some inflammation,

00:04:09.749 --> 00:04:11.979
creating that acute
stress on your muscles,

00:04:11.979 --> 00:04:13.509
on your joints, on your ligaments

00:04:13.509 --> 00:04:14.929
and allowing them to heal.

00:04:14.929 --> 00:04:16.649
You don't want to fall into this bucket

00:04:16.649 --> 00:04:18.449
where you start glorifying pain

00:04:18.449 --> 00:04:21.034
or competing so hard that
you're lifting too much weight

00:04:21.034 --> 00:04:23.243
and you're sacrificing proper form

00:04:23.243 --> 00:04:25.079
and increasing risk of injury.

00:04:25.079 --> 00:04:27.109
I see this all the time with bench press.

00:04:27.109 --> 00:04:29.499
I see guys go into the gym,
they take the bench press

00:04:29.499 --> 00:04:31.429
and they start bouncing
it off their chest.

00:04:31.429 --> 00:04:33.509
They're lifting one side
way more than the other.

00:04:33.509 --> 00:04:37.959
I've literally seen pecs get
torn, rotator cuffs get torn

00:04:37.959 --> 00:04:40.229
in front of me in these exact situations.

00:04:40.229 --> 00:04:42.119
How many times has someone told you

00:04:42.119 --> 00:04:43.319
that you should be stretching?

00:04:43.319 --> 00:04:45.159
Your mom, your dad, grandma,

00:04:45.159 --> 00:04:47.299
the PE teacher, your physical therapist.

00:04:47.299 --> 00:04:50.119
Too often, I see people
doing ballistic stretching.

00:04:50.119 --> 00:04:52.359
What's essentially happening
is you're triggering

00:04:52.359 --> 00:04:54.359
the stretch reflex within the muscle

00:04:54.359 --> 00:04:56.689
that while you're
lengthening it and bouncing,

00:04:56.689 --> 00:04:59.409
it's trying to contract and
shorten and protect itself,

00:04:59.409 --> 00:05:01.049
therefore creating a muscle spasm.

00:05:01.049 --> 00:05:04.069
I tell everyone that they
need to do a good warmup

00:05:04.069 --> 00:05:05.719
before doing any kind of workout

00:05:05.719 --> 00:05:07.839
but it's not mandatory that you stretch.

00:05:07.839 --> 00:05:09.409
You need to get your blood moving,

00:05:09.409 --> 00:05:11.539
the circulation moving,
your heart rate up.

00:05:11.539 --> 00:05:12.689
That's absolutely true,

00:05:12.689 --> 00:05:14.859
but stretching can and should be reserved

00:05:14.859 --> 00:05:16.209
for the end of your workout.

00:05:16.209 --> 00:05:17.929
Or even used as a workout in general.

00:05:17.929 --> 00:05:21.269
I can not begin to tell you
what you're missing out on

00:05:21.269 --> 00:05:22.949
if you do not strength train.

00:05:22.949 --> 00:05:25.459
You actually improve your metabolic rate,

00:05:25.459 --> 00:05:27.569
meaning that you're burning
more calories throughout the day

00:05:27.569 --> 00:05:29.399
'cause you have more
muscle mass to support.

00:05:29.399 --> 00:05:31.059
You're actually burning
more calories as well

00:05:31.059 --> 00:05:32.949
because you're repairing the damage

00:05:32.949 --> 00:05:34.909
that you've caused to your
muscles while working out.

00:05:34.909 --> 00:05:37.289
Remember, this is acute short-term damage

00:05:37.289 --> 00:05:39.689
that is actually beneficial
for your muscles.

00:05:39.689 --> 00:05:42.059
At the same time, you're
strengthening your bones,

00:05:42.059 --> 00:05:44.079
you're improving your hormonal profiles.

00:05:44.079 --> 00:05:46.439
It's just like win, win, win, win, win.

00:05:46.439 --> 00:05:49.629
By only focusing on cardio,
you're just really missing out

00:05:49.629 --> 00:05:52.109
on all of these benefits
for no real reason.

00:05:52.109 --> 00:05:54.229
In fact, newer research suggests that

00:05:54.229 --> 00:05:56.259
ultra performing running athletes

00:05:56.259 --> 00:05:59.029
get a significant benefit
from weightlifting,

00:05:59.029 --> 00:06:02.249
not only in terms of performance
but also injury reduction.

00:06:02.249 --> 00:06:03.869
There is a caveat here, however.

00:06:03.869 --> 00:06:07.089
You should be very careful
of mixing these two workouts

00:06:07.089 --> 00:06:09.429
on the same day or even back to back.

00:06:09.429 --> 00:06:12.459
For example, if you were to
do squats one day very heavy

00:06:12.459 --> 00:06:14.599
and the next day you want
to play pickup basketball

00:06:14.599 --> 00:06:15.749
like I do that quite often.

00:06:15.749 --> 00:06:17.083
If I'm really sore,

00:06:17.083 --> 00:06:19.849
I actually have a much
higher rate of injury

00:06:19.849 --> 00:06:21.619
because the way that your body reacts

00:06:21.619 --> 00:06:23.609
if it falls on an uneven ankle

00:06:23.609 --> 00:06:27.559
or it takes a weird step is by
firing antagonistic muscles,

00:06:27.559 --> 00:06:30.059
opposite muscle groups in
order to protect itself.

00:06:30.059 --> 00:06:31.899
And if your muscles are sore, fatigued

00:06:31.899 --> 00:06:33.259
after a heavy lift session,

00:06:33.259 --> 00:06:34.989
it's more likely that you
could injure yourself.

00:06:34.989 --> 00:06:37.819
One of the most overlooked aspects

00:06:37.819 --> 00:06:40.679
of people's exercise routines is recovery.

00:06:40.679 --> 00:06:44.739
I'm talking adequate rest,
protein intake, sleep, hydration.

00:06:44.739 --> 00:06:47.599
All of this is crucial to
allow your muscles to recover,

00:06:47.599 --> 00:06:49.709
to grow, to decrease injury

00:06:49.709 --> 00:06:51.639
and even giving your brain some time off.

00:06:51.639 --> 00:06:52.869
It's actually been proven

00:06:52.869 --> 00:06:55.209
that when you're over-training
and under sleeping,

00:06:55.209 --> 00:06:58.489
your stress hormones can peak
and stay chronically high,

00:06:58.489 --> 00:07:00.469
which ends up hampering your progress

00:07:00.469 --> 00:07:02.049
and increasing risk of injury.

